<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mikatagahara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mikatagahara was fought on 25 January 1573 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, advancing west, crushed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and his Oda allies, and Ieyasu barely escaped to Hamamatsu Castle. Shingen's death a few months later halted the Takeda advance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mikatagahara.jpg" alt="Battle of Mikatagahara" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa Yoshitora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Mikatagahara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1571 Aoyama Tadamon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, resisted a Takeda invasion of Mikawa by building a series of palisades, but he was later killed by a gunshot. In October 1572 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, having made alliances with the Later Hōjō clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and the Satomi clan of Awa and waited for snow to close the northern passes against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, led 30,000 men south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kofu">Kōfu</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>. Yamagata Masakage led a second force of 5,000 into eastern Mikawa, and the Takeda quickly took Yoshida Castle and Futamata Castle.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> held Hamamatsu Castle with 8,000 men and 3,000 reinforcements sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. Shingen's aim was to save his strength for an attack on Nobunaga and a march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, not to fight Ieyasu. Ieyasu nevertheless refused advice from Nobunaga's officers and his own generals to let the Takeda pass, and drew up his army on the plain of Mikatagahara, just north of Hamamatsu. The historian Kawai Atsushi argued that this was a necessity, since refusing to fight would have cost Ieyasu the respect of the Mikawa samurai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At about four in the afternoon, as snow began to fall, Tokugawa arquebusiers and peasant stone-throwers opened fire. Naitō Masatoyo's cavalry overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a>'s section on the Tokugawa right, and the Takeda horsemen then routed the Oda reinforcements, killing the officer Hirate Hirohide. An attack on the Tokugawa left was held. Shingen then rested his vanguard and sent forward fresh cavalry under Takeda Katsuyori, Obata Masamori and Saegusa Moritomo, followed by the infantry of his main body, and the Tokugawa line broke.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> wished to rescue his trapped general Mizuno Tadashige but was persuaded by his retainer Natsume Yoshinobu to flee. Natsume led a last charge to cover the retreat and was killed, as were Matsudaira Koretada, Naruse Masayoshi and others. Ieyasu reached Hamamatsu Castle with five men and ordered the gates left open and braziers lit. The Takeda vanguard under Baba Nobuharu and Yamagata Masakage suspected a trap and camped for the night.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>That night about one hundred Tokugawa foot soldiers and 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> gunners under Ōkubo Tadayo and Amano Yasukage raided the Takeda camp. Uncertain of Tokugawa strength, and worried that Nobunaga or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> might send help, Shingen withdrew. The tale that the Tokugawa laid a cloth across a gorge to imitate a bridge appears only in later records compiled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, not in contemporary sources, though the site was made a Shizuoka Prefecture Historic Site in 1952.</p><p>The battle was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s most decisive defeat. Shingen was fatally wounded at the siege of Noda Castle in February 1573 and died in May, so no attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mikatagahara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His cavalry and organised vassal bands made Kai a military power, and he crushed Tokugawa Ieyasu at <b>Mikatagahara</b> in 1573.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>He survived defeat by Takeda Shingen at <b>Mikatagahara</b> in 1573 and gained Suruga, Kai and part of Shinano after 1582.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
